      "replacements": [
        {
          "old": "\\begin{abstract}\nWe develop the dynamics of a counted zero pair and its complete exterior\ndivisor for the Newman heat field. Symmetric pair coordinates give an\ninfinite hierarchy of rational moments, regular through mergers and\nalgebraically equivalent to split-pole moments. Indexing those moments by\nadjacent pair produces a coupled zero lattice: shared-root identities,\na common meromorphic resolvent and an exact block flux express the\ncompatibility between neighboring rows. These identities distinguish a\ncommon-divisor reconstruction from independent moment truncations, whose\nsmall residuals need not have small propagated absolute allowances.\nFor one initialized opposite-paired canonical product, we prove a\ncommon-time field approximation with explicit $O(\\log N/N)$ error,\nspatial derivatives and transfer to counted pair coordinates, complete\nfixed-order exterior moments and transport multipliers. A calibrated\nGaussian estimate improves its height dependence while exposing the\nremaining conditioning cost of an absolute error budget.\nComplex powers and damped Cauchy integrals organize the same complete\ninteraction, with explicit divisor and contour errors.\nFor backward gap comparison, we first identify the limitation of initial\ninteraction alone: its canonical high-height allowance is asymptotic to\n$g^2/4$, compared with $g^2/6$ from two neighbors. Retaining the joint\nvariables $V=1-2DM$ and $K=2D^2\\mathcal F$ gives an upper barrier for $V$\nand a lower bound for the squared half-gap. On each fixed positive-time\nslab, a growing canonical neighbor head yields relative loss\n$O(\\Delta/\\log X)$; the source theorem already protects that exterior.\nA separate analytic-residual argument recovers the published high-zero\nvelocity method and permits a matched direct-source comparison.\nTrial-coordinate contour identities yield a local acquisition and\nstopping theorem without assuming exterior reality. Forward block\ndynamics give conditional force contraction and a minimum-gap tail\ncomparison; retaining physical forcing gives an explicit backward\nRiccati comparison. An exact derivative of the initial-interaction\nhorizon identifies what an unchanged forward--backward return loses.\nWe then formulate recursive two-sided refinement through common-source\ntrajectory enclosures and a continuous forward-error/backward-adjoint\nidentity. Sound refinement is distinguished from strict recursive advantage.\nFor direct contour enclosures we prove sharper cardinal interpolation,\nshared-log support and joint annular alias bounds, retaining the full\nquadratic remainder and every source error. These sharpen a relaxation\nof the direct estimator without adding source information.\nThe results are analytical; inherited\ncertificates and fixed scalar arithmetic appear only as supporting\nexamples. No stable short-head closure, new all-height coverage or improved\nde Bruijn--Newman bound is asserted.\n\\end{abstract}",
          "new": "\\begin{abstract}\nWe study a counted zero pair and its complete exterior divisor in the\nNewman heat flow. Symmetric coordinates yield an infinite rational-moment\nhierarchy regular through mergers. Split-pole and complex-power\nrepresentations describe the same interaction, while adjacent pairs form\na coupled lattice with shared-root identities and an exact block flux.\n\nWe construct a compatible finite realization by initializing one\nopposite-paired canonical product and evolving that polynomial over a\ncommon time interval. An explicit $O(\\log N/N)$ estimate controls the\nfield and spatial derivatives. Independently cleared contours transfer\nthis error to pair coordinates, every fixed complete exterior moment,\nforcing and physical transport. A calibrated Gaussian estimate improves\nthe height dependence of the allowance and identifies the conditioning\ncost that can still prevent a useful finite-data budget.\n\nThe gap comparisons distinguish initial interaction from retained\njoint forcing. They give backward barriers, conditional forward\nblock statistics and an exact limitation on an unchanged\nforward--backward return. Common-source enclosures and a continuous\nforward-error/backward-adjoint identity specify what a two-sided\nrefinement must pay. We then prove sharper direct discriminant bounds\nfrom the actual interpolation operator, shared logarithm errors and\njoint annular Fourier aliases. The full quadratic remainder and all\nsource, coordinate, time and rounding errors remain.\n\nThese results separate compatible approximation, useful conditioning and\nimprovement of an outer error relaxation. Numerical certificates serve\nonly as supporting fixed-domain examples. The direct refinements do not\nestablish recursive advantage over equally informed direct methods.\nStable short-head closure, useful uniform conditioning, complete\nall-height continuation and an improved de Bruijn--Newman upper bound\nremain outstanding.\n\\end{abstract}"
        }
      ]
